  "question": "Work, Energy and Power: Define work and write its SI unit.",
  "gold_answer": "# Introduction\n\n---\n\n## Definition\n\nWork is defined as the measure of energy transfer that occurs when an external force acts on an object and causes it to move through a displacement distance. \n\nWork is a scalar quantity calculated as the dot product of the applied force vector and the displacement vector.\n\n---\n\n## Characteristics\n\n* **Directional Orientation Dependency:** Work value can scale as positive, negative, or zero depending on the angle between the force and displacement vectors.\n* **Zero Work Scenarios:** No work is performed if the object remains stationary ($\\Delta x = 0$) or if the applied force vector acts at a right angle ($90^\\circ$) relative to the motion.\n\n---\n\n## Formula\n\nThe mathematical dot product defining mechanical work:\n\n$$W = Fs \\cos(\\theta)$$\n\nWhere:\n* $W$ represents the work scalar value\n* $F$ represents the magnitude of the applied force vector\n* $s$ represents the magnitude of the displacement vector\n* $\\theta$ represents the angle between the force and displacement paths\n\n---\n\n## Metric System Units\n\nThe standard SI unit of work is the **Joule ($\\text{J}$)**, which is defined as the work performed when a constant force of $1 \\text{ Newton}$ displaces an object through a distance of $1 \\text{ meter}$ along its line of action.\n\n$$1 \\text{ J} = 1 \\text{ N} \\cdot \\text{m} = 1 \\text{ kg} \\cdot \\text{m}^2/\\text{s}^2$$\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nWork measures mechanical energy conversion, directly proportional to force intensity and displacement length, with a dependency on their mutual vector alignment angle.",
  "candidate_response": "┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ **Introduction: The Physics of Work**                                                     │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ In everyday language, \"work\" refers to any physical or mental effort. However, in physics, │\n│ work has a very specific definition involving the application of force and the resulting  │\n│ displacement of an object.                                                                │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ **Definition**                                                                            │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Work** is defined as the product of the component of the force in the direction of the  │\n│ displacement and the magnitude of this displacement.                                      │\n│                                                                                           │\n│ *Work is done only when a force applied to an object causes that object to move.*         │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ **Main Formula**                                                                          │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                                                                           │\n│   $$W = F \\cdot d \\cdot \\cos(\\theta)$$                                                    │\n│                                                                                           │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Variables:**                                                                            │\n│ • $W$: Work done                                                                          │\n│ • $F$: Magnitude of force applied                                                         │\n│ • $d$: Magnitude of displacement                                                          │\n│ • $\\theta$: Angle between the force and displacement vectors                              │\n│                                                                                           │\n│ [ 🔬 Open Formula Lab ]                                                                   │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ **SI Unit & Measurement**                                                                 │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ The standard unit for measuring work is the **Joule (J)**.                                │\n│                                                                                           │\n│ > **1 Joule** is defined as the amount of work done when a force of **1 Newton**          │\n│ > displaces an object by **1 meter** in the direction of the force.                       │\n│                                                                                           │\n│ **Dimensional Formula:** $[ML^2T^{-2}]$                                                  │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ **Characteristics of Work**                                                               │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • **Scalar Quantity:** Work has magnitude but no direction.                               │\n│ • **Positive Work:** When force and displacement are in the same direction ($0^\\circ$).   │\n│ • **Negative Work:** When force opposes motion (e.g., friction).                          │\n│ • **Zero Work:** When force is perpendicular to displacement (e.g., carrying a bucket).   │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ **Suggested Questions**                                                                   │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. A man pushes a wall with a force of 100N for 1 hour. How much work is done?            │\n│ 2. Explain why the work done by centripetal force is always zero.                         │\n│ 3. Express 1 Joule in terms of CGS units (Ergs).                                         │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘",
